🔹 Dynamic Method Dispatch (Java)

Dynamic Method Dispatch is a mechanism by which a call to an overridden method is resolved at runtime, not at compile time.

It is one of the core concepts behind Runtime Polymorphism in Java.

✅ Simple Definition (Interview Style)

Dynamic Method Dispatch is the process where a method call is resolved at runtime based on the object being referred to, not the reference type.

🔹 Example in Java
class Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal makes sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Animal obj = new Dog();   // Upcasting
        obj.sound();              // Dynamic Method Dispatch
    }
}
🔹 Output:
Dog barks
🔎 Why?

Reference type → Animal

Object type → Dog

Method executed → Dog’s sound() method

👉 Because Java decides at runtime which overridden method to call.

🔹 Important Points

Only works with method overriding

Only applies to non-static methods

Decision happens at runtime

Based on object type, not reference type

🔹 When It Does NOT Work
❌ Static methods

Static methods are resolved at compile time (Method Hiding).

❌ Variables

Instance variables are resolved based on reference type.

🔹 Real-Life Example (Interview Explanation)

Think of:

Animal a = new Dog();

You are saying:

"I know it's an Animal reference, but at runtime I will decide which real animal it is."

🔹 Difference from Compile-Time Polymorphism
| Feature            | Compile Time   | Runtime     |
| ------------------ | -------------- | ----------- |
| Method Overloading | ✅ Yes          | ❌ No        |
| Method Overriding  | ❌ No           | ✅ Yes       |
| Decision Time      | Compile time   | Runtime     |
| Based On           | Reference type | Object type |

🎯 Interview One-Liner

Dynamic Method Dispatch allows Java to support runtime polymorphism by resolving overridden methods at runtime using the object’s actual type.